WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.740
<v Instructor>So let's talk about the limits</v>

2
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.279
of solution architecture on AWS.

3
00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:06.800
What if you had to handle an extremely high rates

4
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.170
of requests on your infrastructure?

5
00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.360
So let's have a look.

6
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.720
Route 53 is a global DNS service

7
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.330
and it's meant to handle an extremely high rates

8
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.780
of requests so your fine at this stage.

9
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.261
You can handle client, also do some client caching

10
00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:24.600
so to save some data that has been requested from your APIs

11
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.410
in order to save requests going into your infrastructure.

12
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.360
But if your request goes

13
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.690
into your infrastructure, for example, through CloudFront

14
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.630
then CloudFront also has caching capability

15
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.730
and you can achieve easily 100,000 requests per second

16
00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.120
on the CloudFront.

17
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.370
CloudFront has reduction origin

18
00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:41.650
and the origin can do many things

19
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.790
but let's took an example with ALB or API Gateway.

20
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.170
So if you're using an ALB, it can scale tremendously.

21
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.280
And the ALB used to have warmup issues, but not anymore

22
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.660
it can now scale seamlessly.

23
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.800
The API Gateway has a soft limit

24
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.710
of 10,000 requests per second, as we've seen.

25
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.400
And also the API Gateway has some caching capability,

26
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.620
so there's a chance to stop requests at this stage.

27
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.670
The Compute Layer within different options,

28
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.860
if we're using an ASG or ECS on EC2

29
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.120
it's going to be slow,

30
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.670
you're gonna have to bootstrap your instances

31
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.660
after your VMs are created.

32
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.910
So this can take a lot of time to scale

33
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.660
but this still scales well,

34
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.080
it's just going to be a little bit slower.

35
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.110
If you're using Fargate,

36
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.460
it's Docker containers that have to be booted up.

37
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.230
It's going to be quicker to scale.

38
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.930
They've launched onto the AWS Global infrastructure.

39
00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.940
So it may be a little bit faster to scale

40
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.080
and we'll still work with your architecture.

41
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.360
And if you're using Lambda for your Compute Layer,

42
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.410
you can get up to 1,000 concurrent executions

43
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.580
for your Lambda functions in your region,

44
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.230
but this can be increased as well, this is a soft limit.

45
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.230
But it's good for you to see these numbers

46
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.900
just to get understanding of the capability of each side.

47
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.540
So as we can see in the Compute Layer

48
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.980
we don't have any caching capability.

49
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.350
Now we have a Database Layer

50
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.630
which is our data is going to be in RDS, Aurora,

51
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.610
ElasticSearch.

52
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.090
These are going to be provision databases

53
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.590
a lot harder to scale except maybe Aurora,

54
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.490
AWS will disagree with me, but still

55
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.180
you have provisioned a capacity for your database side.

56
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.420
If you're using DynamoDB, then you are

57
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.100
in luck because you can use autoscaling, on-demand scaling.

58
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.530
And so you can enable a much higher read and writes.

59
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.200
If you need some caching capability for your data

60
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.287
then you can have Redis.

61
00:02:27.287 --> 00:02:29.330
You can scale up to 200 nodes.

62
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.980
Memcached, up to 20 nodes in charting

63
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.560
or DynamoDB DAX up to 10 nodes that are primary and replica.

64
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.540
That's going to help cache your DynamoDB queries.

65
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.830
What if you need to store data on disk,

66
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.600
then in case you're using an EBS drive, you get

67
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.600
up to 16,000 IOPS on gp2 and 64,000 IOPS on io1

68
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.490
and it has some caching capability,

69
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.760
that means that you can cache data,

70
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.670
if you wanted to onto an EBS volume,

71
00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.290
that is a very common pattern.

72
00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.740
If you're using Instance Store,

73
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.060
you get millions of IOPS, so a much higher IOPS rates.

74
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.740
And again, your Instance Store can be used

75
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.750
as a local cache for your EC2 instances.

76
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.900
For EFS it's a bit different

77
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.320
because you're sharing files across many instances.

78
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.010
In which case you have two different performance mode,

79
00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.220
General in which we get more performance

80
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.900
the more files we have onto our EFS Network Files System

81
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.230
or we can enable the Max IO

82
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.270
or provisioned IO on EFS, in which we're saying, okay

83
00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.210
we want more IOPS than the number of files we have.

84
00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.100
So please enable that.

85
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.450
Finally, if we need to decouple our applications

86
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.560
then SNS and SQS virtually have unlimited scales.

87
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.570
So we're fine.

88
00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.970
SQS FIFO have about 3,000 requests per second

89
00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.620
with batching or 300 requests per second without batching.

90
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.700
And can you say is something you have to provision?

91
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.690
And for each chart you provision

92
00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.160
you get about one megabytes per second in

93
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.550
and two megabytes per second out per chart.

94
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:52.383
Okay.

95
00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:53.350
Regarding the static routes

96
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.370
we can also have a CloudFront edge at the edge

97
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.994
and we can use S3

98
00:03:57.994 --> 00:04:01.170
and S3 as we seen the main lines performance is 3,500 puts

99
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.460
and 5,500 gets per pre fixed per second.

100
00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.300
And the KMS limits will be a problem

101
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.660
if you're using SSE KMS encryption

102
00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.920
in which case you'll be limited

103
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.740
to 10,000 API calls on KMS, for example, per region.

104
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.170
Now this is a number that changes based

105
00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:17.390
on the region you're choosing

106
00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.830
but I just wanna give you some idea that KMS

107
00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.420
will be a limit for your S3 performance.

108
00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.700
And obviously you can have your EC2 instance

109
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.920
where your Compute Layer also talk to S3.

110
00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.120
It doesn't have to be through CloudFronts.

111
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.440
So one which you notice is that the more we are going

112
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.750
on the right hand side in this architecture

113
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.550
the more it's going to be expensive.

114
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.850
So the earlier we can cache the request

115
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:40.240
the less expensive is going to be for us.

116
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.310
So all the things we can have to do with this is caching

117
00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.970
on all left hand side, maybe deal with TTL,

118
00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.350
understand all the network calls that have to be made,

119
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.470
if we have to go all the way to the right,

120
00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.730
understand the computations that need to happen,

121
00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.250
the cost of these computations that need to happen

122
00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.550
and also the increased latency from going

123
00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.340
and propagating a request all the way to the right.

124
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.800
So this is a high level solution architecture discussion,

125
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:03.870
but it's really important for you

126
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.960
to see all the limits of AWS into one slide.

127
00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.490
Just so you understand why caching is so important,

128
00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.000
why it's so much better to treat the requests

129
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.210
at the edge versus treating them at the

130
00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.750
very very deep parts of your architectures

131
00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.940
and why caching can be made in so many different places.

132
00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:21.773
Okay.

133
00:05:21.773 --> 00:05:22.606
So, that's it for this lecture.

134
00:05:22.606 --> 00:05:23.439
I hope you liked it,

135
00:05:23.439 --> 00:05:25.170
and I will see you in the next lecture.

